        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the determinant of a square matrix: the factor by which it scales areas (in 2-D) or volumes (in 3-D). 2 means everything comes out twice the size; 1 means size is kept (a rotation); 0 means space is flattened onto a line or plane - the matrix cannot be undone; a negative number means it is also mirrored.\n\nUse it to test whether a matrix can be inverted, whether a transform mirrors (a rotation estimated from noisy data with det -1 is a reflection), or how much a mapping stretches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.det\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA square matrix, or a stack of them (one determinant each). Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndeterminant:\nThe determinant.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2 0, 0 3 stretches by 2 one way and 3 the other: areas grow 6 times. 1 2, 2 4 has a second row twice the first, so it flattens the plane onto a line: 0 (shown as -0.000: the arithmetic lands on zero from just below). 0 1, 1 0 swaps x and y, a mirror: -1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.det is the numpy version. t.linalg.inv inverts a matrix whose determinant is not 0. t.linalg.eig gives the separate stretch factors, whose product is the determinant.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the determinant of a square matrix: the factor by which it scales areas (in 2-D) or volumes (in 3-D). 2 means everything comes out twice the size; 1 means size is kept (a rotation); 0 means space is flattened onto a line or plane - the matrix cannot be undone; a negative number means it is also mirrored.\n\nUse it to test whether a matrix can be inverted, whether a transform mirrors (a rotation estimated from noisy data with det -1 is a reflection), or how much a mapping stretches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.det\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA square matrix, or a stack of them (one determinant each). Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndeterminant:\nThe determinant.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2 0, 0 3 stretches by 2 one way and 3 the other: areas grow 6 times. 1 2, 2 4 has a second row twice the first, so it flattens the plane onto a line: 0 (shown as -0.000: the arithmetic lands on zero from just below). 0 1, 1 0 swaps x and y, a mirror: -1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.det is the numpy version. t.linalg.inv inverts a matrix whose determinant is not 0. t.linalg.eig gives the separate stretch factors, whose product is the determinant."
        },
